WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.323
(rocket blasting)

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.523
- This is the world's most
powerful rocket taking off.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.366
And this is it exploding shortly after.

00:00:13.366 --> 00:00:14.880
(crowd groaning)

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.400
After years of development,
Space X's Starship,

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.700
and its super heavy rocket
with its 33 raptor engines

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.510
finally got airborne in April,
for a short while at least.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.450
Now, while SpaceX has launched
hundreds of spaceships,

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.700
it's Starship that the
company hopes will become

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.370
a rapidly reusable gateway into space.

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.310
But while it may have taken
years to get even to this point,

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.040
it's actually just the very
beginning of Starships long road

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.840
to carry humans to another planet.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.550
So what's the journey ahead for Starship

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.300
and what will it take to get there?

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.130
SpaceX say the idea behind Starship

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:50.130
and the super heavy is to
be the first fully reusable

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.370
space transportation system
where both the spaceship

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:56.610
and the rocket powering it can
be used over and over again.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:58.500
One of the most prominent
missions for Starship

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.720
will be to carry astronauts
from a spacecraft

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.720
orbiting the moon, down to
the lunar surface and back,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.780
as part of NASA's Artemis
3 moon landing missions.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:09.810
I caught up with WSJ space
reporter, Michah Maidenberg.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.290
- NASA awarded a nearly
$3 billion contract

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.420
for the Artemis 3 Moon landing.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.083
That's currently scheduled for,
you know, sometime in 2025.

00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:21.429
2025 is coming up pretty quickly.

00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:23.460
For SpaceX, it's a very high profile

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.880
and intense objective
that they have to reach.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.700
- SpaceX is also planning on
using Starship to carry cargo

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.770
into space, whether that be the companys

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.980
starlink satellites or
fuel for other Starships

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.440
and SpaceX CEO, Elon Musk has also said

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.740
that one day he hopes to use
Starship for a mission to Mars.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.690
But building something this complex

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.630
that's fully reusable isn't exactly easy.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.560
As Musk said at WSJ's CEO council in 2021.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.220
- Man, Starship is a hard,
hard, hard, hard project.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:54.960
It is so preposterously difficult

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.760
that there are times where I wonder

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.360
whether we can actually do this.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.350
- So what did we learn from the launch?

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.110
Well, while there have been
some tests of Starship before,

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:05.880
this is the first time it's been powered

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:09.390
by the massive super heavy
rocket system, and the company

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:12.030
wasn't exactly sure how
successful the launch would be.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.430
- [Kate] If we lift off and clear the pad,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:15.873
we're calling that a win.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.540
- [George] Things seem to be going well

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.880
for the first few minutes.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.080
Although it appeared that some of

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.640
the raptor engines were out.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.860
The super heavy rocket
also failed to separate

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.723
from Starship, and then.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.930
However, the launch will
have left the company

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.460
with a trove of data.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.070
- You know, one of the
main goals of this flight

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.990
was simply to get information.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.780
The way SpaceX historically has operated

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.470
is when it learns something,
it uses that to try to

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.400
quickly make changes,
improvements to iterate.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.230
- So what's it going
to take to get Starship

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:50.970
from this to this?

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.580
Well, SpaceX will need
a lot more test flights,

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.970
as NASA's moon mission could
be as soon as two years away.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.850
- Gwen Shotwell, the
company's longtime president

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.980
said a couple months ago at a conference,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.650
she wants to fly a lot
of flights with Starship,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.310
starting with the company's
own Starlink satellites

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:11.520
because they need to figure
out what this vehicle,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.840
what this machine can do.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.610
- One thing that's almost
certain is that NASA

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.800
will be watching those
flight tests closely.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.050
The agency has said they're
not going to use Starship

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:22.170
to carry humans until there
have been successful tests

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.870
and that SpaceX will conduct
an uncrewed demonstration

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.540
to the moon prior to the Artemis missions.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.870
- So in a way, right now,
one of the big next steps

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.230
for the Artemis program
is for SpaceX to show

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.470
that it can operate Starship the way

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:38.940
it's like laid out to do.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.550
- So, while the launch might
have been one giant leap

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.627
for Starship, it's just
one small step for SpaceX.

00:03:45.688 --> 00:03:48.374
(dramatic music)

